From c4130baf23252d522b24d555befe8fe44033371d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lzwei196 <lzweijinwei196@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 11:51:49 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 02/11] KI file tools: review r1 fixes (junctions on Python
 3.11, safe globs, big KIs, hints keep the KI)

- Junctions: checked through the lstat reparse tag (IO_REPARSE_TAG_MOUNT_POINT), which
  Python 3.11 has; the Windows app builds on 3.11, where os.path.isjunction does not exist.
  A folder link or junction is shown as "(folder link)" and never walked, counted or searched.
- Globs: a * followed by fixed text and another wildcard takes that text at its first place
  (atomic group); ** at most 2 and * at most 16 per pattern. Same answers as the reviewed web
  translation (checked on 20,000+ generated cases), and '*a'*15+'*b' is now instant.
- Big KIs: a path pattern walks only the folder its fixed start names; a missing file is looked
  for in its own folder first; a cut walk says only that some matches may be missing; one
  listing counts at most 50,000 files in all, and only the folders it shows; hint lists capped.
- Hints keep the KI: every suggested call carries ki="..." when one was given, and intake paths
  keep their catalogue prefix (Demo/...), so a hint followed word for word reaches the same KI.
- Arguments: absolute subdir/path (/x, \x, C:\, C:x, UNC) refused before trimming; non-text
  subdir/path/pattern get a clear error.
- Tests: paged read metadata, absolute and non-text arguments, a junction without
  os.path.isjunction, glob equivalence and speed, big-KI search, ki/intake hints.
